# hard-stop-over-graceful-degradation

When a planning system encounters an unmet hard constraint — insufficient inventory, missing required data, contradictory status — the correct response is an explicit, named halt. Gracefully degrading (producing a reduced plan, skipping a required element, improvising values) masks the violation and perpetuates operational errors downstream. Halt loudly; do not silently produce a diminished success.

## Procedures
- Enumerate hard constraints before generating any plan output.
- If any hard constraint is unmet, emit a named halt signal identifying the violated constraint.
- Do not produce a partial or reduced plan as a fallback — produce no plan at all.
- Require explicit resolution (restocked inventory, corrected status, new confirmed data) before retrying.

